    "body": "> I seem to be unable to reproduce this by deleting the directory starship is in.\r\n> Could you please create a new issue with steps to reproduce it, if possible? This seems to be a similar but separate issue.\r\n\r\nIn terminal A:\r\n```\r\n$ mkdir aaa\r\n$ cd aaa\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen terminal B:\r\n```\r\n$ rmdir aaa\r\n```\r\n\r\nBack in terminal A:\r\n```\r\n$ ls\r\nls: reading directory '.': No such file or directory\r\nthread 'main' panicked at 'Unable to identify current directory: Os { code: 2, kind: NotFound, message: \"No such file or directory\" }', src/context.rs:69:51\r\nnote: run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote that  I need to actually run something (`ls` or `cd` for instance) for the crash to actually happen. \r\n\r\n> This happens to me every time Starship is upgraded by `homebrew` - I have to close all my local terminals and reopen.\r\n\r\nI've installed it for the first time today. This is in Archlinux, using the 'community/starship-0.51.0-2' package.",
